A||||19||GT Bastard||Mindless destruction of Fiero's...||||4||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||02-05-2000||10:28 PM||||I think Kit-Cars are a terrible waste of a good fiero. I just can't fathom the idea of chopping up a nice GT to make a fake foreign car.  Whazzup wit dat? I was just wonding if anybody felt the same...  how many Fiero's do you think have been lost to kit cars?||||reg||4
Z||000001||fiero56||02-05-2000||10:50 PM||||I don't mind if the Fiero can easily be changed back over to stock form. Just little changes I can take, but not when everything is pulled just for the space frame. Or when they "ruin" the frame or other parts for cosmetics. Reminds me of animal testing. Don't know why, but it just popped in my mind.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||02-06-2000||01:14 AM||||I renewed my interest in Fieros when I saw a photo of a Lambo conversion, and then a friend told me that there were lots of body kits for Fieros. I started doing research and discovered the Finale, and decided I wanted it. Still do..<P>However, my goals have changed slightly since then. I now want a fastback (86 or 87) GT with IRM-style sidescoops, tom's decklid scoop, RS Motorsports hood scoops, and a custom interior.. I'd like to build a Finale completely from scratch, with a tube steel chassis, corvette axles, brakes, steering, maybe a corvette engine as well, but ultimately a twin turbo Northstar.. All wheel drive, power steering, and a computer controlled braking system like the new DeVilles, Aleros and Carerras. (probably more, but those are the only ones I can think of at the moment.)<P>I don't think I'll have this car for a long time...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>But I'd like to have my slightly modified GT this year..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gokart||02-06-2000||06:50 AM||||Better becoming a kit car I guess than rotting away in some boneyard with all the parts we Fiero owners could use. I went on <A HREF="http://www.junkyarddog.com" TARGET=_blank>www.junkyarddog.com</A>  and got every boneyard in my state and I'm working on calling every one of them to see if they have Fieros. I'm on vacation next week and may visit a few for misc. stuff. Maybe I should bring flowers as well as tools!! LOL!||||reg||9
Z||000004||William Federle||02-06-2000||12:13 PM||||I guess that the re-body kits I like the best are the ones where you can still see the Fiero lines.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||02-06-2000||06:21 PM||||I don't see anything wrong with re-bodying (it's a new word) a Fiero, but it would seem silly to use a GT unless the body has already been trashed.  There are tons of less desirable body styles to choose from (although I like all the Fiero body styles).  for example:  If you want the '88 suspension, try a Formula instead of a GT.  Or waste an '88 GT body and send it to me!||||reg||1
Z||000006||TOM||02-06-2000||10:09 PM||||speaking of "lost" fiero's. What about the 8 or so I see go to the crusher a week. Those are lost forever.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Greg Piet||02-07-2000||03:40 PM||||There are oodles of donor cars out there.  Those donor cars get all the useful stuff stripped off them so we all can use their parts.  I think that if no Fiero's were stripped at the boneyard, or turned into donor cars we would be having a bear of a time finding replacement parts (I mean a company isnt going to make a mold to produce aftermarket replacement fenders for a fiero, although they make them for old mustangs, etc...) ||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||02-07-2000||05:16 PM||||I think you guys just need a new perspective. Look first at what makes a car valuable. Sure rareity and functionality are big, good looks and brand uniqueness are also important. But you need to also look at how the platform was used. To be accepted as a MAJOR player in the kit car biz means that our little car will forever be remembered. And, as long as there is interest, there should be parts (at least the mechanical parts). <P>Look at the VW. That car hasn't been imported into this country since the 70's. But, you can build an entire car from parts found in catalogs. It has reached true "cult" status (I saw a restored 60 something sun roof car go for $11,500 at a Kruze auto auction last year). I think the Fiero is headed the same direction. <P>As far a GTs getting hacked up and made into kit cars... cool! Beats the crusher. My GT was a prime candidate for either. It may yet be a "kit" of some kind. Most likly kit is for the power plant. I really want a 3800 SC Gen II motor in it. I can vision a new red paint job on the stock body with red leather seats and custom dash, red carpet and a kickin' sound system. So, does this mean that I'm wrong?? After all, it won't be anywhere near original again if I go this route.<P>I also have a couple of real classic cars: 69 Firebird 400 convertible, 65 Nova SS, 68 RS Camaro, and 69 Firebird coupe. Are all of these valuable collector cars?? NO! All are beautiful cars but none particually rare. They all have their flaws. Just like my 87 GT with 109k miles, faded Med red metallic paint and ragged tan interior... thay made THOUSANDS of them. Will it be worth a bunch of money some day? I don't think so. You can still buy a real 69 RS Convertible for less than $12,000.<P>Now, on the flip side, I'm trying to get my hands on a 88 GT, Yellow, Wing, bass booster, 5 speed, and T-Tops with 85k miles. the car is perfect except for the clearcoat on the wing and thin P-strips on the T-Top. Now that car is worth collecting. And, maybe in a few more years it'll be considered a great car like the Buick Gran National, or the Seleen Mustang etc. But if I'm waiting on the insestment to double, I better get it cheap or be better off buying Home Depot stock. In the mean time, the more uses they find for our little cars, the better. It can only make them more valuable in the long run.<P>Anyway, that's my soap box, I feel better now (flame suit "ON"),<P>Roy L.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Spyder||02-07-2000||05:22 PM||||I’m not sure how many of you know this: <BR>There are a lot of us rebodiers (new word?) on this forum. There is a lot of knowledge that we supply on this forum, because we have dismantled and rebuilt.  I gave away the parts I didn’t need anymore. I have bought all kinds of performance parts which helps the demand for more performance parts to be made so that everyone can benefit from the useless destruction of a good Fiero.  If I had not bought my donor Fiero, it was destined to have a miserable short lived life.  It was in terrible shape. I am giving it a new Pampered Life with so much Strength and Bad A$$ Attitude that it would never have had. <BR>Also, since I know and love these cars so much, I am searching for a GT to restore to original.<BR>PS: I respect  everyone's personal views and I do not take it personally.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eric||02-07-2000||06:50 PM||||I got a little itch to build a kitcar out of a Fiero a while back, but then I decided it would be much better to tear apart a Mustang for a Cobra kit instead.  I don't like Mustangs much.  They don't look very attractive.  Which is probably the Fiero kitcar builder rational too.  Not to say they don't like the overall car, just the looks.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||02-07-2000||07:58 PM||||mrfixit58--I'd enjoy seeing some pics of your other "classic" cars.  If you don't feel like posting them here due to the nature of the forum (Fiero based), feel free to email them to me.  Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Capt Fiero||02-07-2000||08:17 PM||||Ya know I hate the idea of tearing up good looking fieros for kit cars, but I have learned the good side to people that do.  A friend of mine builds Lambos for a busniess, and you know what he was doing with all the extra stuff, this is going to hurt so be prepaired, he was paying a guy to pick them up and take the parts to the dump, seats hoods, interiors you name it, he was actually paying to have the stuff thrown in the dump, oh it hurts just to think about it. Then I found him, I know pay him to store the parts until I can get over and get them, he no longer rips and tears the parts off, I pay almost nothing for the parts, he gives me most of the little stuff.  I then bring them home, store them and use the money to support our local Fiero Club, I sell the parts at 1/2 of wrecking yard prices so everyone is happy, I make a few bucks the club makes a few bucks and Harry no longer pays to throw away Fiero Parts.<P>David||||reg||3
Z||000013||87GT||02-07-2000||09:05 PM||||I often wonder how many fieros are still being driven on the road and are in their original style?  ||||reg||5
Z||000014||Monkeyman||02-08-2000||12:22 AM||||My '86 is both.||||reg||1
Z||000015||87GTBro||02-08-2000||12:32 AM||||I am partial to the GT Fastback design.  If I had a SE or similar, I wouldn't mind putting a body kit on it.<P>But look at the bright side of all this.  Because a lot of the stock Fieros are turning into kit cars, that makes the originals that much more rare and, therefore, valuable.  I'm holding onto my 86 GT.||||reg||14
Z||000016||HotFieroGT||02-13-2000||01:56 PM||||Ok,<P>I am reading this thread and keep hearing about all the parts people are throwing away and cars being crushed etc.  I have been trying to get a left rear taillight and the spring that holds up the decklid (attached to left side) for an 88GT since I started reading this forum.  Nobody ever seems to have them or respond to my inquiries.  I'm having a little trouble believeing there are all these parts out there!  ||||reg||8
Z||000017||mshill||02-13-2000||05:50 PM||||I have said this before, my 86SE was in such good shape cosmetically, I couldn't cut it up to make my kit.  I found a totally trashed 86GT (headed for the crusher) to use as my kit donor.  So I am retoring the mechanicals on the SE prior to starting my kit.<P>I feel bad about the GT, but the exterior is in pretty bad shape.||||reg||1
Z||000018||dogtired||02-15-2000||05:09 PM||||Had varying difficulities over the years with the same question!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>I have heard folks when trying to sell a car, say that they wouldn't sell it to a particular individual if they were going to race it, re-body it, trick paint it, part it out (insert most anything here)??  So I guess it does depend on ones perspective!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>But I have also sold enough vehicles over the years to know when you have a buyer standing there with the money, you had better take it??<BR>Don't take me wrong here, I do not like seeing perfectly good cars going to its demise for nothing! But if I sell a car, the buyer has the right FREEDOM to do with it whatever they wish.<BR>I certainly am not wealthy enough to save them all. So if some have to go to the crusher, (and I have sent some), or into kit cars (some of which are real COOL) they do in a way make mine more valuable as an original well kept specimen??? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>If I had 100 acres and big buildings, with lots of money, I still couldn't save them all. There is a point that transporting them across country isn't worth it just to save a generic used up coupe! SO just try and save all you can and bow your head every time you hear of one going to its death. But keep the faith knowing that you helped by keeping yours in good shape!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000019||Phil||02-15-2000||07:19 PM||||If I didn't start to build a kit car I wouldn't now own two other Fieros. Whilst cutting up an 85GT for the basis of my Lambo I became impressed with the design of these cars and started to dig further into their history and so forth. Cutting up a <BR>Fiero caused me to restore an 88GT and hot rod an 88 Formula.  I never really looked too hard at Fieros before, test drove an 84 when they first came out but I had an 81 RX7 at the time. Needless to say I didn't buy the Fiero. So one sacrificed 85 saved two 88s plus a couple of you people got some parts.||||reg||1
